# Git Fork vs Clone

## Clone

Copies a repo to your local machine. You push directly back to the **same repo**.

```
GitHub repo  →  clone  →  local repo  →  push  →  same GitHub repo
```

```bash
git clone https://github.com/username/my-repo.git
# make changes
git push origin main  # pushes back to the original repo
```

Used when you **have write access** to the repo (your own repo or a team repo).

---

## Fork

Copies someone else's repo into **your own GitHub account**, then you clone your fork. You push to your fork, not the original.

```
original repo  →  fork  →  your GitHub copy  →  clone  →  local  →  push  →  your GitHub copy
```

```bash
# 1. fork on GitHub (click Fork button)

# 2. clone YOUR fork
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_username/their-repo.git

# 3. make changes and push to YOUR fork
git push origin main

# 4. open a Pull Request → asking the original repo to accept your changes
```

Used when you **don't have write access** — open source contributions, for example.

---

## Key Difference

| | Clone | Fork |
|---|---|---|
| Where you push | Original repo | Your copy on GitHub |
| Need write access | ✅ Yes | ❌ No |
| Used for | Team projects, your own repos | Open source contributions |
| Pull Request needed | Usually no | Yes, to contribute back |

---

## Fork + Upstream Pattern

When you fork, it's common to also track the **original repo** as `upstream` so you can pull in new changes from it:

```bash
# after cloning your fork, add the original as upstream
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original-owner/their-repo.git

# check your remotes
git remote -v
# origin    https://github.com/YOUR_username/their-repo.git (your fork)
# upstream  https://github.com/original-owner/their-repo.git (original)

# pull latest changes from original repo
git fetch upstream
git merge upstream/main

# push updated code to your fork
git push origin main
```

```
original repo (upstream)  →  fetch/merge  →  your local  →  push  →  your fork (origin)
```

This keeps your fork in sync with the original repo.

---

## Typical Open Source Workflow

```bash
# 1. fork on GitHub

# 2. clone your fork
git clone https://github.com/YOUR_username/their-repo.git
cd their-repo

# 3. add upstream
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original-owner/their-repo.git

# 4. create a feature branch
git checkout -b my-feature

# 5. make changes and commit
git add .
git commit -m "Add my feature"

# 6. push to your fork
git push origin my-feature

# 7. open a Pull Request on GitHub
# your fork → their repo
```

---

## Quick Reference

```bash
# clone a repo
git clone https://github.com/username/repo.git

# add upstream after forking
git remote add upstream https://github.com/original/repo.git

# sync fork with original
git fetch upstream
git merge upstream/main
git push origin main

# check all remotes
git remote -v
```

---

## Golden Rule

> **Clone** when you have write access. **Fork** when you don't.
